#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Tell a deferred bootc apply apart from a failed one, and clean up only the
deferral (RT #1594, RT #1566).

Started only by `OnFailure=` on bootc-fetch-apply-updates.service, which is
also the only unit this looks at. Background in
/etc/systemd/system/bootc-fetch-apply-updates.service.d/10-defer-for-backups.conf.

Why this exists at all. The interlock works -- a pbs rotation holds a
`--mode=block` shutdown inhibitor, logind refuses the reboot `bootc upgrade
--apply` asks for, the drop-in's TimeoutStartSec cuts the resulting park and
OnFailure= arms a retry. But every deferral cycle leaves failed units behind:
the apply unit itself with `Result=timeout`, plus one failed transient per
refused reboot (`run-p220259-i220260.service`, running bootc's
`systemctl reboot --message=Initiated by bootc`). Four accumulated on gloval
on 2026-11-09 and the "Host failed systemd units" check sat CRITICAL for two
hours *because the interlock was doing its job*. A check that fires when
nothing is wrong gets acknowledged, and then it is not a check any more.

So the host must stop reporting itself broken when it is not -- without
getting quieter about the case where it really is. Everything here turns on
that one distinction, and the bias is deliberate: anything this cannot
positively identify as a deferral is left failed, loudly, exactly as today.

THE TEST. A deferral is a reboot that was asked for and refused. Three facts,
all required:

  1. The apply unit's `Result` is `timeout`. That is the drop-in's
     TimeoutStartSec cutting bootc's park, which is the only way a refused
     reboot ends -- bootc calls systemd-run without --wait, never sees
     `systemctl reboot` exit non-zero, and waits forever for a SIGTERM that
     is not coming. A real apply failure ends `exit-code`; a SUCCESSFUL apply
     ends `signal`, because the reboot it got tears the unit down and its own
     process takes the SIGTERM (that last one happens on every good apply and
     fires this unit too, so it has to be excluded by name, not by luck).

  2. A failed transient service from THIS run of the apply unit whose command
     is bootc's reboot (`systemctl show` again). That is the reboot request's
     own wreckage: the transient exists because bootc asked for a reboot, and
     it is in `failed` rather than gone because `systemctl reboot` came back
     non-zero.

  3. logind's denial recorded in this cycle's journal -- "Access denied", or
     the interactive-authentication form -- read from bootc's own units (the
     apply unit and this run's reboot transients) over a twenty-minute window
     that covers one cycle and clears the previous one. Fact 2 says the reboot
     did not happen; this says it did not happen because it was REFUSED.

Why fact 3 exists. `systemctl reboot` can fail for reasons that are nothing to
do with a backup, and those are real failures. Without this, a host whose
reboot kept failing on its own account would be swept clean every thirty
minutes, never update, and never say so -- the exact silence this change is
supposed to be the opposite of.

Why fact 3 is not the obvious test, "a block shutdown inhibitor is held right
now". That is the right cause read ten minutes after its own effect, because
TimeoutStartSec is 10min, and it is wrong in both directions. It is absent
when the rotation ended inside that window: on 2026-11-09 the refusal was at
02:19:06 and the rotation finished at 02:31:16, with the timeout landing about
02:28:46 -- two and a half minutes short of losing the evidence, and one
slower start from reading a genuine deferral as a failure. And it is present
when an unrelated rotation merely started after a reboot had already failed
for its own reasons, which would sweep a real failure. So the inhibitor is
read from logind's own BlockInhibited property and logged with whoever holds
it, because it is what a person wants to see on a Saturday night, and it
decides nothing.

A diagnostic string is a thin thing to rest on, and it rests the right way up:
no denial matched means real failure, and the units stay exactly as they are
today. If the wording ever changes, this stops cleaning up and says so in the
journal, which is the direction every uncertainty here is pointed.

Nothing here is enabled or scheduled, and it starts no apply. The one thing
it can set in motion is bootc-apply-retry.timer, which the drop-in's
OnFailure= already armed directly before this unit existed.

Usage:
  bootc-apply-deferred                 classify this failure and act on it
  bootc-apply-deferred --dry-run       classify the live host, change nothing
  bootc-apply-deferred --decide-from F classify the state in JSON file F and
                                       print the decision, touching no unit
  bootc-apply-deferred --self-test D   check the subprocess wrapper, then
                                       classify every fixture in directory D
                                       against its own recorded expectation.

The last two exist because this decides when to erase the evidence of a
failure, and "looks right" is not evidence for that. The image build runs
--self-test over /usr/share/bootc-apply-deferred/testdata, whose fixtures
cover a refusal, a refusal whose rotation ended before the timeout, both of
logind's refusal wordings, a real exit-code failure, a successful apply's
teardown, a timeout with no reboot attempted, a reboot that failed without
being refused while an unrelated rotation was running, a denial logged by some
other operation in the same cycle, a block inhibitor with nothing in the
journal, an empty journal, a stale transient from an earlier cycle, a failed
transient belonging to something else, and a deferral that has to clean up one
while leaving the other. Each fixture is a host state with the
expected decision beside it, and they stay in the image so the same check can
be re-run on a host.
"""

import json
import os
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys

APPLY_UNIT = "bootc-fetch-apply-updates.service"
RETRY_TIMER = "bootc-apply-retry.timer"

# Every substring must appear for a transient to be attributed to bootc's
# reboot. "Initiated by bootc" is bootc's own --message and nothing else on
# this host writes it; the other two keep a different bootc-authored command
# from matching. Deliberately substrings and not an exact command line:
# systemd's rendering of ExecStart carries the argv[0] override and the
# quoting of --message, and this must not turn into a test of that rendering.
BOOTC_REBOOT_MARKERS = ("systemctl", "reboot", "Initiated by bootc")

# How many block-mode inhibitor rows to put in the journal. The interesting
# case is one pbs rotation; this is a bound on a log line, not a limit on the
# decision, which never reads the table at all.
MAX_INHIBITOR_ROWS = 8

# What a refused reboot says. `systemctl reboot` writes logind's refusal to
# stderr, and gloval recorded "Call to Reboot failed: Access denied" on
# 2026-11-09. The second is logind's other refusal shape, for a caller it wants
# to authenticate rather than one it rejects outright.
DENIAL_MARKERS = ("Access denied", "Interactive authentication required")

# How far back to look for that denial. Derived, not guessed: a cycle cannot
# last longer than the drop-in's TimeoutStartSec of 10min plus teardown, and
# bootc-apply-retry.timer's OnActiveSec puts consecutive cycles at least 30min
# apart, so twenty minutes covers this cycle with slack at both ends and still
# clears the previous one. A relative window rather than the apply unit's own
# start converted to a date: systemd reports that start on CLOCK_MONOTONIC,
# and converting it drifts by however long the host has been suspended --
# measured at five minutes on a laptop, which would put the window AFTER the
# refusal and lose it.
#
# Nothing at runtime can notice if either of those two settings moves and
# leaves this stale, so the image build asserts all three together.
CYCLE_WINDOW = "-20min"

# Read from the failing unit and from each candidate transient. Result is the
# classification; ActiveState confirms the unit is still failed rather than
# already restarted under us; Transient keeps this away from anything
# installed on disk; InactiveExitTimestampMonotonic is the start of the
# current run, which is what attributes a transient to this cycle.
APPLY_PROPERTIES = ("ActiveState", "Result", "InactiveExitTimestampMonotonic")
TRANSIENT_PROPERTIES = (
    "ActiveState",
    "Transient",
    "ExecStart",
    "InactiveExitTimestampMonotonic",
)


def log(msg):
    """Journal, via stdout. systemd captures it under this unit's name."""
    print(f"bootc-apply-deferred: {msg}", flush=True)


def invoke(argv, check=False):
    """Run argv and return (returncode, stdout, stderr).

    The whole outcome, because one caller has to tell "this command does not
    support that option" apart from "there was nothing to find" and those
    share an exit status. check turns any failure into an exception, named, so
    that the journal distinguishes a reset that would not happen from a retry
    that could not be armed.
    """
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            argv, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
            text=True, timeout=30,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as err:
        if check:
            raise RuntimeError(f"{' '.join(argv)} could not run: {err}") from err
        return 127, "", str(err)
    if check and proc.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"{' '.join(argv)} exited {proc.returncode}: "
            f"{proc.stderr.strip() or proc.stdout.strip()}"
        )
    return proc.returncode, proc.stdout, proc.stderr


def run(argv, check=False):
    """Return stdout, or "" if the command is missing or fails.

    A missing or broken helper must not be able to turn a real failure into a
    deferral: every caller treats "" as absence of evidence, and absence of
    evidence classifies as a real failure.
    """
    returncode, out, err = invoke(argv, check)
    if returncode != 0:
        log(f"{' '.join(argv)} exited {returncode}: {err.strip()}")
        return ""
    return out


def show(units, properties):
    """`systemctl show` several units at once, keyed by unit name.

    One invocation for all of them, not one per unit: this runs on a host that
    by definition has failed units, and the count is whatever Nagios is
    complaining about.

    Keyed by the Id property rather than by the position of each block, so the
    result does not depend on systemctl emitting blocks in argument order.
    Not --value either: ExecStart's value is a brace-delimited struct, and
    positional output would be ambiguous the moment a property was absent.
    """
    units = list(units)
    if not units:
        return {}
    wanted = ("Id", *properties)
    out = run(["systemctl", "show", "--no-pager",
               *(f"--property={p}" for p in wanted), *units])
    found = {}
    for block in out.split("\n\n"):
        props = {}
        for line in block.splitlines():
            key, _, value = line.partition("=")
            if key:
                props[key] = value
        name = props.get("Id")
        if name:
            props["unit"] = name
            found[name] = props
    return found


def monotonic(value):
    """A systemd *TimestampMonotonic property as an int, or None.

    0 means "never", which systemd reports for a unit that has not run this
    boot. Both timestamps compared here come from the current boot, so a
    monotonic clock is the right one -- it does not move when the realtime
    clock does.
    """
    try:
        usec = int(value)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None
    return usec if usec > 0 else None


def failed_services():
    """Names of every service unit currently in the failed state."""
    out = run(["systemctl", "list-units", "--type=service", "--state=failed",
               "--all", "--plain", "--no-legend", "--no-pager"])
    names = []
    for line in out.splitlines():
        fields = line.split()
        if fields and fields[0].endswith(".service"):
            names.append(fields[0])
    return names


def block_inhibited():
    """logind's BlockInhibited property, e.g. "shutdown:sleep", or "".

    This is the aggregate logind consults when it decides to refuse a reboot,
    so it is the same fact from the same source rather than a reconstruction
    of it from the table `systemd-inhibit --list` prints for people.
    """
    out = run(["busctl", "--json=short", "get-property",
               "org.freedesktop.login1", "/org/freedesktop/login1",
               "org.freedesktop.login1.Manager", "BlockInhibited"])
    if not out.strip():
        return ""
    try:
        return json.loads(out).get("data", "") or ""
    except (ValueError, AttributeError):
        log(f"could not parse BlockInhibited: {out.strip()!r}")
        return ""


def inhibitor_table():
    """The block-mode rows of `systemd-inhibit --list`, for the journal only.

    BlockInhibited above says *that* shutdown is blocked; this says who is
    blocking it, which is what a person reading the journal next Saturday
    wants to see. It never feeds the decision.

    Only the rows whose MODE is a block, and only a few of them. The delay
    inhibitors a host registers as a matter of course are not what refused the
    reboot, and copying every one of them plus its free-text reason into the
    journal on every cycle buries the one line that matters. MODE is the last
    column, which is all this needs to know about the table's shape -- the
    decision reads logind's property instead, precisely so that nothing
    load-bearing depends on parsing this.
    """
    if not shutil.which("systemd-inhibit"):
        return ""
    rows = [line for line in run(["systemd-inhibit", "--list", "--no-pager"]).splitlines()
            if line.rstrip().endswith(("block", "block-weak"))]
    return "\n".join(rows[:MAX_INHIBITOR_ROWS])


def denial_in(text):
    """True when journal text carries logind's refusal of a REBOOT.

    The one piece of evidence that says the reboot was refused rather than
    merely unsuccessful, which is the difference between a backup holding an
    update off and a host that cannot reboot. A diagnostic string is a thin
    thing to rest a decision on, so it rests the right way up: no match means
    real failure, and the units stay as they are.

    Line by line, and the line has to name the reboot as well as the denial.
    gloval logged "Call to Reboot failed: Access denied", which is systemctl's
    "Call to %s failed: %s" with logind's method in it -- so the method name is
    not pinned (RebootWithFlags is the same message), but a denial that does
    not mention a reboot at all belongs to some other operation and is not
    evidence about this one.
    """
    return any(
        "reboot" in line.lower() and any(m in line for m in DENIAL_MARKERS)
        for line in text.splitlines()
    )


def cycle_journal(units):
    """The journal of these units over the window of the current cycle.

    One call for all of them -- repeated --unit= is ORed -- and bounded at
    both ends. The units are bootc's own: the apply unit, because bootc may
    have relayed the refusal through its own stderr, and the transients
    `systemd-run` made for the reboot, because that is where
    `systemctl reboot` writes by default. Which of the two carries it is not
    something that can be established from here, so both are read.

    --since is what keeps this a statement about THIS cycle. Without it, one
    genuine deferral earlier in the same boot would leave a denial in the
    apply unit's journal that corroborated every real failure after it for the
    rest of the boot. See CYCLE_WINDOW for where the twenty minutes comes
    from.

    --grep rather than --lines for the second bound. A line cap keeps the most
    RECENT entries, so a chatty cycle would push the refusal out of view and
    read as a real failure; asking journalctl for the matching lines bounds
    the output without being able to hide the one line that matters. The
    markers hold no regex metacharacters, and journalctl's smart case makes an
    uppercase pattern case-sensitive, so this matches what denial_in() matches
    -- which still has the last word on the result, because --grep is an
    optimisation here and not the test.

    The unfiltered read behind it covers a journalctl built without PCRE2,
    which refuses --grep with the same exit status it uses for "nothing
    matched" -- so the two are told apart by whether anything was written to
    stderr, and the second read happens only on a complaint, not on every
    real failure. Without it, such a host would run this fix forever while it
    quietly did nothing.
    """
    if not units:
        return ""
    query = ["journalctl", "--no-pager", "--quiet", "--output=cat",
             f"--since={CYCLE_WINDOW}", *(f"--unit={u}" for u in units)]
    returncode, out, err = invoke([*query, f"--grep={'|'.join(DENIAL_MARKERS)}"])
    if returncode == 0 or not err.strip():
        return out
    log(f"journalctl refused --grep ({err.strip()}); reading the window unfiltered")
    return run(query)


def gather():
    """Collect everything the classifier needs from the live host."""
    apply_props = show([APPLY_UNIT], APPLY_PROPERTIES).get(APPLY_UNIT, {})
    transients = list(show(failed_services(), TRANSIENT_PROPERTIES).values())
    # Only the units that could possibly matter. Reading the journal of every
    # failed service on the host to answer a question about bootc's reboot
    # would be work for its own sake.
    candidates = [p["unit"] for p in transients
                  if p.get("ActiveState") == "failed"
                  and p.get("Transient") == "yes"
                  and is_bootc_reboot(p)]
    return {
        "apply": apply_props,
        "block_inhibited": block_inhibited(),
        "inhibitor_table": inhibitor_table(),
        "transients": transients,
        "journal": cycle_journal([APPLY_UNIT, *candidates]),
    }


def shutdown_blocked(block_inhibited_value):
    """True when a block inhibitor currently covers shutdown.

    Split on the separator rather than searching for a substring: the what
    list is colon-separated ("shutdown:sleep:idle") and a substring test would
    be a sieve the day a new what contains this one's name.
    """
    return "shutdown" in [w for w in (block_inhibited_value or "").split(":") if w]


def is_bootc_reboot(props):
    """True when this transient's command is bootc's reboot.

    ExecStart and nothing else. Description is tempting, because systemd-run
    defaults it to the same command line and a second source looks like
    insurance against systemd rendering ExecStart differently one day -- but a
    description is a label anyone can set to anything, and this function
    decides which failed units get their failure erased. That has to turn on
    what the unit ran, not on what it was called. ExecStart's rendering is
    stable enough to rest on: whatever the struct around it looks like, it
    carries the full argv, which is where all three markers live.
    """
    command = props.get("ExecStart", "")
    return bool(command) and all(marker in command for marker in BOOTC_REBOOT_MARKERS)


def classify(state):
    """Return (is_deferral, notes, units_to_reset).

    notes is the reasoning, in order, for the journal. units_to_reset is empty
    unless this is a deferral -- a real failure's state is never touched.
    """
    notes = []
    apply_props = state.get("apply") or {}
    result = apply_props.get("Result", "")
    active = apply_props.get("ActiveState", "")
    apply_started = monotonic(apply_props.get("InactiveExitTimestampMonotonic"))

    whats = state.get("block_inhibited", "") or "none"
    blocked = shutdown_blocked(state.get("block_inhibited"))
    if blocked:
        notes.append(f"a block inhibitor covers shutdown right now ({whats})")
    else:
        notes.append(f"no block inhibitor covers shutdown right now ({whats})")

    bootc_transients, foreign = [], []
    for props in state.get("transients") or []:
        if props.get("unit") == APPLY_UNIT:
            continue
        if props.get("ActiveState") != "failed":
            continue
        # Transient=yes, not a name pattern. A unit installed under /etc or
        # /usr is somebody's deliberate configuration and is never ours to
        # reset, however it is named.
        if props.get("Transient") != "yes":
            continue
        if is_bootc_reboot(props):
            bootc_transients.append(props)
        else:
            foreign.append(props)

    if foreign:
        notes.append(
            "leaving %d failed transient(s) that are not bootc's reboot: %s"
            % (len(foreign), ", ".join(p.get("unit", "?") for p in foreign))
        )

    this_cycle = []
    if apply_started is not None:
        for props in bootc_transients:
            started = monotonic(props.get("InactiveExitTimestampMonotonic"))
            if started is not None and started >= apply_started:
                this_cycle.append(props)

    # Fact 1. Anything but a timeout is not a refused reboot: exit-code is a
    # real apply failure, and signal is what a successful apply leaves behind
    # on the way down, which also fires this unit.
    if active != "failed":
        notes.append(f"{APPLY_UNIT} is {active or 'unknown'}, not failed")
        return False, notes, []
    if result != "timeout":
        notes.append(
            f"{APPLY_UNIT} Result={result or 'unknown'}, not timeout"
            + (" (the shape of a successful apply, not a refusal)"
               if result == "signal" else "")
        )
        return False, notes, []
    notes.append(f"{APPLY_UNIT} Result=timeout")

    # Fact 2. The park has to be explained by a reboot that was asked for and
    # refused. Without that artefact this is a timeout from something else --
    # a hung fetch, say -- and hiding those is how an interlock becomes a way
    # to never hear about broken updates again.
    if apply_started is None:
        notes.append(
            f"{APPLY_UNIT} has no start timestamp this boot, so no transient "
            "can be attributed to this run"
        )
        return False, notes, []
    if not this_cycle:
        notes.append(
            "no failed transient from this run is running bootc's reboot"
            + (" (%d older one(s) exist and are left alone)" % len(bootc_transients)
               if bootc_transients else "")
        )
        return False, notes, []
    notes.append(
        "bootc asked for a reboot in this run and did not get it: %s"
        % ", ".join(p.get("unit", "?") for p in this_cycle)
    )

    # Fact 3. The reboot must have been REFUSED, not merely unsuccessful.
    # `systemctl reboot` can fail for reasons that are nothing to do with a
    # backup, and those are real failures: a host whose reboot keeps failing
    # on its own account would otherwise be swept clean every thirty minutes,
    # never update, and never say so.
    #
    # The live inhibitor cannot carry this. It is the right cause, but it is
    # read ten minutes after the effect -- TimeoutStartSec is 10min -- so it
    # is both absent when a rotation has already ended and present when an
    # unrelated rotation started after the reboot failed for its own reasons.
    # It is logged above because it is what a person wants to see, and it
    # decides nothing. The denial recorded during this cycle is the refusal
    # itself, and that is what this turns on.
    if not denial_in(state.get("journal", "") or ""):
        notes.append(
            "no refusal recorded in this cycle's journal, so the reboot "
            "failed for its own reasons rather than being refused"
        )
        return False, notes, []
    notes.append("this cycle's journal records the refusal")

    # All three hold. Reset every failed transient attributable to bootc's
    # reboot, not only this run's: each one is the wreckage of a refusal by
    # the same test, and a backlog from before this unit shipped should not
    # need a person with a keyboard either.
    reset = [APPLY_UNIT] + [p["unit"] for p in bootc_transients]
    return True, notes, reset


def check_invoke():
    """Check invoke()'s four outcomes against real commands.

    No mocking: `true`, `false` and a path that does not exist cover success,
    a non-zero exit and a failure to execute, which is all this wrapper has to
    get right. It is worth checking because every classification path reads
    "" as absence of evidence and therefore as a real failure, so a wrapper
    that returned output on a failed command, or swallowed a failure under
    check, would move the bias in the one direction it must never move.

    Returns a list of complaints, empty when all four hold.
    """
    missing = "/nonexistent/bootc-apply-deferred-self-test"
    cases = []

    returncode, out, _ = invoke(["true"])
    if (returncode, out) != (0, ""):
        cases.append(f"invoke(true) gave {(returncode, out)!r}")

    returncode, out, _ = invoke(["false"])
    if returncode == 0:
        cases.append("invoke(false) reported success")
    if run(["false"]) != "":
        cases.append("run(false) returned output")

    returncode, _, err = invoke([missing])
    if returncode == 0 or not err:
        cases.append(f"invoke(missing) gave {(returncode, err)!r}")

    for argv in (["false"], [missing]):
        try:
            invoke(argv, check=True)
        except RuntimeError as caught:
            if argv[0] not in str(caught):
                cases.append(f"check=True on {argv[0]} did not name it: {caught}")
        else:
            cases.append(f"check=True on {argv[0]} did not raise")

    return cases


def self_test(directory):
    """Run classify() over every fixture in directory and check the result.

    Each fixture carries its own "expect": {"deferral": bool, "reset": [...]},
    which classify() never reads. Keeping the state and the expectation in one
    file means a fixture cannot drift away from what it was written to prove.
    """
    failures = 0
    for complaint in check_invoke():
        print(f"FAIL invoke(): {complaint}", file=sys.stderr)
        failures += 1
    if not failures:
        print("ok   invoke(): success, non-zero exit, missing command, check=True")

    names = sorted(n for n in os.listdir(directory) if n.endswith(".json"))
    if not names:
        print(f"bootc-apply-deferred self-test: no fixtures in {directory}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    for name in names:
        with open(os.path.join(directory, name), encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            fixture = json.load(handle)
        expect = fixture.get("expect")
        if not isinstance(expect, dict):
            print(f"FAIL {name}: fixture records no expectation", file=sys.stderr)
            failures += 1
            continue
        deferral, notes, reset = classify(fixture)
        got = {"deferral": deferral, "reset": sorted(reset)}
        want = {"deferral": expect.get("deferral"),
                "reset": sorted(expect.get("reset", []))}
        if got == want:
            print(f"ok   {name}: {'deferral' if deferral else 'real failure'}")
        else:
            failures += 1
            print(f"FAIL {name}: expected {want}, got {got}", file=sys.stderr)
            for note in notes:
                print(f"       {note}", file=sys.stderr)
    if failures:
        print(f"bootc-apply-deferred self-test: {failures} check(s) failed",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print(f"bootc-apply-deferred self-test: {len(names)} fixtures passed")
    return 0


def main(argv):
    dry_run = "--dry-run" in argv

    if "--self-test" in argv:
        return self_test(argv[argv.index("--self-test") + 1])

    if "--decide-from" in argv:
        path = argv[argv.index("--decide-from") + 1]
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            state = json.load(handle)
        deferral, notes, reset = classify(state)
        print(json.dumps(
            {"deferral": deferral, "reset": reset, "notes": notes},
            indent=2, sort_keys=True,
        ))
        return 0

    state = gather()
    table = state.get("inhibitor_table", "").strip()
    if table:
        log("block-mode inhibitors held:")
        for line in table.splitlines():
            log(f"  {line.rstrip()}")

    deferral, notes, reset = classify(state)
    for note in notes:
        log(note)

    if not deferral:
        # Nothing is reset, on purpose. The failed units stay exactly as they
        # are so that a person and the monitor both still see them, which is
        # the whole point of not teaching the monitor to look away.
        log("classified as a REAL failure: leaving every failed unit as it is")
    else:
        log("classified as a DEFERRAL: clearing %d failed unit(s): %s"
            % (len(reset), ", ".join(reset)))
        if not dry_run:
            # One invocation for the lot. It also means a unit that refuses to
            # reset fails this unit rather than leaving a half-cleaned host
            # that reports itself clean.
            run(["systemctl", "reset-failed", *reset], check=True)
            log("reset-failed done")

    # Armed in both branches, which is what the drop-in's OnFailure= did
    # before this unit stood between them -- the defect being fixed is the
    # failed units, not the retry policy. It is also the right call on a real
    # failure: the common one is a transient fetch error, and one attempt
    # thirty minutes out is what fixes it without anyone being woken up. The
    # failed unit is what gets a person involved when it does not; the retry
    # is what means nobody had to be.
    if dry_run:
        log(f"would start {RETRY_TIMER}")
        return 0

    # Blocking, not --no-block, so a retry that could not be armed fails THIS
    # unit and is seen. It cannot deadlock on the start job it is waiting for:
    # the timer has no ordering relation to this service in either direction,
    # and an ordering dependency only holds between jobs in one transaction,
    # which this separate `systemctl start` is not part of. The unit still
    # carries a short TimeoutStartSec so that reasoning being wrong costs two
    # minutes rather than the host's update path.
    run(["systemctl", "start", RETRY_TIMER], check=True)
    log(f"armed {RETRY_TIMER}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except Exception as err:  # noqa: BLE001 - the message belongs in the journal
        log(f"error: {err}")
        sys.exit(1)
